<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">January <a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau&quot; (&quot;The Old Land of My Fathers&quot;, usually rendered &quot;Land of My Fathers&quot;) was written in January 1856 by Evan James, with a tune by his son James James, both of Pontypridd in Glamorgan; the earliest written copy survives in the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau</b>&quot; (&quot;The Old Land of My Fathers&quot;, usually rendered &quot;Land of My Fathers&quot;) was written in January 1856 by Evan James, with a tune by his son James James, both of Pontypridd in Glamorgan; the earliest written copy survives in the National Library of Wales.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hen-wlad-fy-nhadau">Hen Wlad Fy Nhadau</a></li></ul></section>
